{"items":[{"tags":["ansible","devops","ansible-2.x"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":79897467,"is_answered":false,"question_id":74024709,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1772157056,"creation_date":1772157056,"body":"TL;DR\nAFAICT, all config file settings that resolve to a path are relative to the\nconfiguration file except those found in . However, all\npath settings may include embedded environment variables.\nGiven  and vault password file(s) live in :\n\nSet an environment variable with the config file directory:\n\nor\n\n\nAnd embed the variable in the  path(s) of :\n\n\n\nPath Settings\nConfiguration setting specifications are found in  which is\nread by  to populate the configuration\nsettings before execution. For each setting, specifications include:\n\n - type describing how the setting should be interpreted,\n  - configuration file section and key,\n  - environment variable(s) that may contain the setting, and\n - Ansible variable(s) that may contain the setting.\n\nWhen a setting of type , , or  is loaded from an\nenvironment variable or Ansible variable, the path(s) are resolved relative to\nthe current working directory.\nWhen loaded from a configuration file,  passes the configuration\nfile directory as  to the  function and path(s)\nare resolved relative to .\nFor example, in , the  key of the \nsetting is  and the  value is . So, a relative\nfile path is resolved to the configuration file directory.\nThe Problem\nThe configuration setting , with the  key of\n, has a  value of . So, file paths are not\nresolved when the config file is read. Whether loaded from a\nconfiguration file or environment variable,  will\nbe a list of Vault IDs in the format of , where  may be\nan unresolved file path.\nThe base for all Ansible commands is the  module, which\ncomplements  with any  command-line\noptions. For each Vault ID, it calls  to read the\npassword from . Unfortunately, the origin of ,\nbe it from the configuration file or environment variable, is not known by\n so it resolves relative paths from the current working directory.\nThe Workaround\nWhile it doesn&#39;t appear to be well documented, all path configuration settings\nget resolved in , including Vault ID\nsource.  resolves the user home directory (~) and any embedded\nenvironment variables, allowing for the workaround described, above.\nThe Solution\nSince the only time it is known if a configuration setting comes from the\nconfig file is when the file is read, a new setting  should be added to\n to resolve Vault ID source\npaths from  the same way other path settings in the config\nfile are resolved. Changing the  for  in\n to the new type would then fix the issue.\nHere is a working example of the needed code change in :\n\n","excerpt":"The Workaround\nWhile it doesn&#39;t appear to be well documented, all path configuration settings\nget resolved in ansible.utils.path.unfrackpath(), including Vault ID\nsource. <span class=\"highlight\">unfrackpath</span>() resolves the user &hellip; ","title":"Ansible configuration: Paths not relative to ansible.cfg?"},{"tags":["python","ansible","dependencies","yum","dnf"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":69751148,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1636035148,"creation_date":1635411707,"body":"Were trying to execute following Ansible playbook:\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\nEverything works perfect until the last block (yum).\nNo matter what am I doing to the Python version, this is the error I am getting:\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\nI&#39;ve tried using multiple package managers(e.g. ASDF, PYENV etc.) , python versions, downloading the missing &quot;dnf module&quot; and pointing at it via hosts file, or changing\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\nTo auto, auto_legacy, pointing at python location, at exactly were previously downloaded python-dnf modules stores etc. etc.\nI am struggling with that issue already for 2 weeks, all the other SOF and other sites googling does not resolve the issue by any kind.\nAt the given moment I am using Python via Anaconda.\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\nAlso tried adding, &quot;use_backend=&quot;(all of the possible versions of it).\nAfter modifying access right to the hosts file and changing it contents, I am getting this:\nTraceback (most recent call last): File &quot;/Users/artyom/opt/anaconda3/bin/ansible-playbook&quot;, line 62, in import ansible.constants as C File &quot;/Users/artyom/.local/lib/python3.8/site-packages/ansible/constants.py&quot;, line 181, in config = ConfigManager() File &quot;/Users/artyom/.local/lib/python3.8/site-packages/ansible/config/manager.py&quot;, line 302, in init self._config_file = find_ini_config_file(self.WARNINGS) File &quot;/Users/artyom/.local/lib/python3.8/site-packages/ansible/config/manager.py&quot;, line 244, in find_ini_config_file potential_paths.append(unfrackpath(&quot;~/.ansible.cfg&quot;, follow=False)) File &quot;/Users/artyom/.local/lib/python3.8/site-packages/ansible/utils/path.py&quot;, line 50, in unfrackpath b_basedir = to_bytes(os.getcwd(), errors=&#39;surrogate_or_strict&#39;) PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied\n","excerpt":"_config_file = find_ini_config_file(self.WARNINGS) File &quot;/Users/artyom/.local/lib/python3.8/site-packages/ansible/config/manager.py&quot;, line 244, in find_ini_config_file potential_paths.append(<span class=\"highlight\">unfrackpath</span> &hellip; (&quot;~/.ansible.cfg&quot;, follow=False)) File &quot;/Users/artyom/.local/lib/python3.8/site-packages/ansible/utils/path.py&quot;, line 50, in <span class=\"highlight\">unfrackpath</span> b_basedir = to_bytes(os.getcwd(), errors=&#39;surrogate_or_strict&#39;) &hellip; ","title":"Unable to push YUM commands towards cloud VM via Ansible (MacOS)"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":247}